    explanation: "Net operating income, almost always written as NOI, is the bedrock number in commercial and rental real estate. A net operating income calculator subtracts a property's operating expenses from its gross operating income to reveal the profit the building produces from operations alone, before any mortgage payment or income tax. Nearly every other valuation metric, from cap rate to debt coverage, is built on top of this single figure, which is why getting it right matters so much.\n\nGross operating income is the money the property actually brings in: rent collected after allowing for vacancy, plus extras like parking, laundry, or storage fees. Operating expenses are the recurring costs of keeping it running: property taxes, insurance, maintenance and repairs, management fees, and utilities the owner covers. Crucially, NOI excludes financing costs and capital expenditures, because those depend on the buyer and distort a property-to-property comparison.\n\nThe calculation works because it separates the performance of the asset from the way any particular owner finances or is taxed on it. Two investors with very different loans see the same NOI on the same building, which is what makes it a fair basis for valuation. Lenders use it to size loans and check debt coverage, appraisers use it to derive value from cap rates, and investors use it as the starting point for judging whether a deal's operations can support its price and its debt.",
