    formula: "Net proceeds = sale price − mortgage balance − (sale price × commission%) − other closing costs",
    formulaExplanation: "Starting from the sale price, the calculator subtracts three things that stand between the headline number and your bank account: the outstanding mortgage balance you must repay, the agent commission computed as a percentage of the sale price, and any other closing costs such as transfer taxes and title fees. What remains is your net proceeds, the cash you actually take home. The total selling costs figure sums the commission and closing costs so you can see exactly how much the sale itself consumes.",
    explanation: "The sale price on a for-sale sign is rarely the amount a seller actually pockets. A seller net proceeds calculator bridges that gap by subtracting everything that comes out of the sale before you get paid: the remaining mortgage balance, the real estate agent commission, and other closing costs like transfer taxes and title fees. The result is your net proceeds, the real cash you walk away with, along with a clear tally of what the sale itself costs you.\n\nThe biggest deductions are usually the loan payoff and the commission. Whatever you still owe on the mortgage must be settled at closing from the sale price, and agent commissions, often around five to six percent split between the buyer's and seller's agents, take a meaningful slice off the top. Layer on closing costs and the difference between the sale price and your take-home can be tens of thousands of dollars, which surprises many first-time sellers.\n\nThe calculation works by starting from the gross sale price and peeling away each obligation in turn until only your money is left. Seeing the breakdown early helps you price realistically, judge whether an offer clears your remaining loan with room to spare, and plan the down payment on your next home. It also makes the cost of selling visible, which is useful when weighing whether to sell now, negotiate a lower commission, or wait for more equity to build.",
